class:: Izhikevich
summary:: Membrane, recovery, and spike outputs from an Izhikevich neuron
categories:: UGens>Generators>Chaotic>Flows
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/RulkovMap

description::

The Izhikevich model is a continuous-time system of ordinary differential equations (ODEs) that accurately and efficiently simulates the spiking and bursting dynamics of biological cortical neurons. By adjusting its parameters, it can reliably mimic regular spiking, intrinsic bursting, or chattering neuron types. Conceptually, this brings biological neural behavior directly to the audio server, proving incredibly useful for synthesizing organic clicks, neural crackle, or driving generative rhythmic events through biologically grounded physics.

Implements Eugene Izhikevich's 2003 two-state spiking-neuron model. Voltage uses two half Euler steps, recovery uses one full update, and reaching 30 mV applies code::v = c:: and code::u = u+d:: while emitting a one-calculation spike.

code::timeScale:: is model milliseconds per audio second, so 1000 runs in real time. Like link::Classes/Trig1::, the third output is a one-calculation event; unlike Trig1, it is generated by integrated neuron state.

subsection:: Dynamics view

image::izhikevich-dynamics.png#Left: membrane voltage over model time. Right: membrane v against recovery u. The figure uses the regular-spiking defaults with input = 12 so repeated reset cycles are visible.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: input
Applied model current.
argument:: a
Recovery time-scale parameter.
argument:: b
Recovery sensitivity parameter.
argument:: c
After-spike voltage reset in millivolts.
argument:: d
After-spike recovery increment.
argument:: v0
Initial membrane voltage.
argument:: u0
Initial recovery state.
argument:: timeScale
Model milliseconds per audio second; nonpositive values hold.
argument:: substeps
Init-rate integration subdivisions, clipped to 1 through 16.
argument:: reset
Restore initial state and suppress a spike on that calculation.
returns:: Membrane v, recovery u, and spike trigger.

method:: kr
Control-rate version; spikes remain one control block wide.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
{ Izhikevich.ar[2] * 0.1 }.play
)
::

code::
(
{
    var spike = Izhikevich.ar(input: 12)[2];
    Decay2.ar(spike, 0.001, 0.04) * SinOsc.ar(800) * 0.1
}.play
)
::

code::
// Biological Cortical Chattering
(
{
    var current, sig, spike, freq, out;
    // Input current modulates the neuron's firing rate
    current = MouseX.kr(2, 15);
    // Continuous-time simulation of neuron bursting dynamics
    sig = Izhikevich.ar(input: current, a: 0.02, b: 0.2, c: -50, d: 2, timeScale: 1000);
    // The raw membrane potential rests at -50mV and spikes to +30mV
    // Detect the spikes to trigger a physical model
    spike = sig > 0; 
    freq = sig.linexp(-70, 30, 200, 1500); // Sub-threshold voltage wiggles pitch
    out = Ringz.ar(spike, freq, 0.05);
    Pan2.ar(out * 0.05)
}.play;
)
::





subsection:: Real-time v-u portrait

The helper receives all three UGen outputs: v and u draw the trail, while the spike output drives the sound.

code::
(
SDPhaseView.open(
    "Izhikevich neuron: v-u portrait",
    [-70, 32], [-15, 20],
    { Izhikevich.kr(input: 12) },
    { |state|
        Pan2.ar(
            Decay2.ar(K2A.ar(state[2]), 0.001, 0.05) * SinOsc.ar(800, 0, 0.1),
            state[0].clip(-70, 32).linlin(-70, 32, -1, 1)
        )
    }
);
)
::
